# PHASE 4 – SQL JOINS

# 20. SQL Joins

Joins combine data from multiple tables using related columns.

Consider two tables:

### Employees

| emp_id | name | dept_id |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Ankit | 10 |
| 2 | Rahul | 20 |
| 3 | Amit | 10 |
| 4 | Priya | 30 |
| 5 | Karan | NULL |

### Departments

| dept_id | dept_name |
|---|---|
| 10 | IT |
| 20 | HR |
| 30 | Finance |
| 40 | Marketing |

## 1. INNER JOIN

Returns matching records from both tables.

```sql
SELECT
    e.name,
    d.dept_name
FROM employees e
INNER JOIN departments d
    ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;
```

Result: Employees with matching department IDs.

## 2. LEFT JOIN

Returns all records from the left table and matching records from the right table.

```sql
SELECT
    e.name,
    d.dept_name
FROM employees e
LEFT JOIN departments d
    ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;
```

Employees without matching departments still appear. Their department name will be NULL.

## 3. RIGHT JOIN

Returns all records from the right table and matching records from the left table.

```sql
SELECT
    e.name,
    d.dept_name
FROM employees e
RIGHT JOIN departments d
    ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;
```

All departments appear, including departments without employees.

## 4. FULL OUTER JOIN

Returns matching and non-matching rows from both tables.

```sql
SELECT
    e.name,
    d.dept_name
FROM employees e
FULL OUTER JOIN departments d
    ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id;
```

Note: MySQL does not directly support FULL OUTER JOIN; it can be emulated using LEFT JOIN and RIGHT JOIN with UNION.

## 5. CROSS JOIN

Returns every possible combination of rows from both tables.

```sql
SELECT
    e.name,
    d.dept_name
FROM employees e
CROSS JOIN departments d;
```

## 6. SELF JOIN

Joins a table with itself.

Example: Employees and their managers.

```sql
SELECT
    e.name AS employee,
    m.name AS manager
FROM employees e
LEFT JOIN employees m
    ON e.manager_id = m.emp_id;
```

This example assumes a `manager_id` column exists.

## Join Summary

| Join | Result |
|---|---|
| INNER JOIN | Matching rows |
| LEFT JOIN | All left + matching right |
| RIGHT JOIN | All right + matching left |
| FULL OUTER JOIN | All rows from both sides |
| CROSS JOIN | Cartesian combinations |
| SELF JOIN | Table joined with itself |

### One-Minute Revision

- Joins combine related tables.
- The ON condition specifies how rows match.
- LEFT JOIN preserves every row from the left table.
- INNER JOIN keeps matching rows.
- Missing matches can produce NULL values.

